<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Puruhá</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A highland people of central Ecuador around Chimborazo who were absorbed by the Incas and later by the Spanish, but whose identity survives today.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Puruhá occupied the highland basin around the volcano <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a>, in the modern provinces of Chimborazo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivar-province-ecuador">Bolívar</a>, from around 800. Archaeology shows a sequence of local pottery traditions, with the Puruhá phase often linked to tombs found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their political organisation beyond a series of chiefdoms under local lords. They farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and quinoa on the volcanic slopes and herded llamas. Their tombs contained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> ornaments and distinctive pottery, including vessels with modelled human faces. Later chroniclers claimed that Puruhá lords allied by marriage with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, but these accounts are doubtful.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Puruhá were brought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule around 1470 during the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</a>. After the Spanish conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riobamba">Riobamba</a> was founded in their territory in 1534. Their descendants, Kichwa-speaking communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chimborazo">Chimborazo</a>, keep a strong indigenous identity.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/puruha.jpg" alt="Puruhá" width="320" height="481"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernando_Daquilema.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1470 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Local cults</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Puruhá</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ecuador">Ecuador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Puruha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1135&amp;focus=puruha" aria-label="Puruhá on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2514 2232 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Puruhá lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3027 2937h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2834 2488h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2834 2488h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ecuador. <a href="/atlas?year=1135&amp;focus=puruha">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

